    "q-183381": {
      "dom_id": "step-q183381",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14179858/assets/b1532a8be2697c516d88c262ef2d61429f76d1f16a9137c7203de2ca8b451a37.png)\n\nThe shaded regions above represent the truth sets of predicates $P(x)$ and $Q(x),$ defined over the universal set ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following is the truth set of $P(x)⇔Q(x)?$",
      "worked_solution": "Since we have that \n\n$$\nP(x)⇔Q(x)≡(P(x)∧Q(x))∨(¬P(x)∧¬Q(x)),\n$$\n\n \n\nthe corresponding truth set can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P⇔Q}=({T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q})\\cup ({\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we sketch the set ${T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14179858/assets/f14a8b0a78145e74cd030e2ea648e74167d1b1c4915c0f1fd97ffc1db0a82430.png)\n\nThen, we sketch the set ${\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}$ and ${\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14179858/assets/bf67afedc00989576903dca9330f636a997f903b722328dec84db7f847925fce.png)\n\nAs a result, ${\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q}$ is the following: \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14179858/assets/cabd18cef1dd073877772c85c3ccec372a9f9658d71d3c278fb673489229657e.png)\n\nFinally, to get the truth set of $P(x)⇔Q(x),$ we take all the points that belong to ${T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q}$ or ${\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14179858/assets/74d8f90fd8cdfe0e92c5cdf2adc2565fb52bf23ba38a0e6ce492f0c85d5ed796.png)",
